Prometheus Architecture

🔹 What is it? 🏗️
 Prometheus follows a simple yet powerful architecture to collect, store, and query metrics.
 It works by pulling data from different sources and storing it as time-series data.

🔹 Why it matters in real-world? 🌍
 Understanding architecture helps you:
 ✔ Troubleshoot issues
 ✔ Scale monitoring systems
 ✔ Design production-ready setups
👉 Without this, you’re just using the tool—not mastering it.

🔹 Core Components of Prometheus: ⚡
✔ Prometheus Server 🖥️
Main component
Collects (scrapes) metrics
Stores data
Runs queries
✔ Targets 🎯
Systems/apps from which metrics are collected
Example: Servers, containers, APIs
✔ Exporters 📦
Convert system data into Prometheus format
Example: Node Exporter (CPU, memory metrics)
👉 App → Exporter → Prometheus
✔ Time-Series Database (TSDB) 📈
Stores metrics with timestamps
Helps track changes over time
Enables historical analysis

🔹 How It Works (Flow): 🔄
Prometheus server sends request
Targets (via exporters) expose metrics
Server collects (scrapes) data
Data stored in TSDB
Queries used for dashboards & alerts

🔹 Real-world Example: 💡
You have a web application running on servers
👉 Exporter collects:
CPU usage
Memory usage
👉 Prometheus server pulls data every 15 seconds
 👉 Stores it in TSDB
 👉 If CPU > 90% 🚨 → Alert triggered

🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ Server = Brain 🧠
 ✔ Targets = Data sources 🎯
 ✔ Exporters = Data converters 📦
 ✔ TSDB = Storage 📈
 ✔ Pull model = Data collection 🔄
